WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:05.105
<i> NARRATOR: The China Seas.</i>

00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:10.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Ringed by the countries
 of southeast Asia,
 these are some of the</i>

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.847 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> most fiercely contested
 waters on earth.</i>

00:00:18.884 --> 00:00:23.155
Beneath the waves,
the shattered remains
of epic battles.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:26.225 align:start size:91% position:9%
JAMES: It's as if you took
hundreds of ships and put
them through a blender.

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:32.098 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Mysterious treasures, and
 technological wonders all</i>

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.402
<i> hidden from view...</i>

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:39.305
<i> until now.</i>

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:45.211 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Imagine if we could
 empty the oceans, letting
 the water drain away to</i>

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:48.848
<i> reveal the secrets
 of the sea floor.</i>

00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:52.951
<i> Now, we can.</i>

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:57.757 align:start position:0%
<i>Using the latest
underwater scanning technology,</i>

00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:04.230 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> piercing the deep oceans
 and turning accurate
 data into 3D images.</i>

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:12.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> How did America defeat
 the biggest battleship
 of World War II?</i>

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:13.806
JAMES: It was huge.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:15.841
It was powerful, it was fast.

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:19.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
For the Americans
facing it, it was a
brute of a battleship.

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:22.648 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: What's the
 truth behind the strange
 disappearance of the</i>

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:26.552 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> mightiest invasion force
 of ancient times?</i>

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:30.856 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIC: One of the biggest
disasters in the history
of maritime warfare.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:35.895
<i> NARRATOR: And how do you
 protect the Worldwide Web
 from the perils of nature?</i>

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.952
<i> To compete or collaborate?</i>

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.455
<i> Trade or invade?</i>

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:07.560 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> These twin human
 instincts have defined the
 China Seas for centuries.</i>

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:12.398 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> As these turbulent waters
 begin to drain away,</i>

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:17.704
<i> 140 miles south of Japan, they
reveal a top-secret battleship.</i>

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.908
<i> The biggest ever put to sea.</i>

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.511
<i> What were its secrets?</i>

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:29.782
<i> And why couldn't they save
 the lives of 3,000 men?</i>

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:40.593 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> October 1944,
 two giant warships sail
 towards the biggest</i>

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:43.463
<i> naval battle in history.</i>

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:48.902 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> The mighty Yamato and
 its twin, Musashi.</i>

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:53.206 line:0%
-The size of the Yamato
is, is breathtaking.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:56.108 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
The Yamato and
its sister ship, Musashi,
were by far the biggest

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.144 line:0%
battleships ever built.

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:03.916
<i> NARRATOR: Almost
 900 feet long,
 127 feet wide,</i>

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.921
<i> these super battleships
 dwarf their opponents.</i>

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:16.196 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Their hulls are protected
 by an incredible
 16 inches of armor plate.</i>

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.802
<i> And every part of them
 bristles with weapons.</i>

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:30.075
<i> On the bow, two giant turrets.</i>

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:33.012
<i> Then over 100 smaller guns.</i>

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:36.616
<i> Before a third turret.</i>

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> They are the biggest
 floating fortresses
 ever put to sea.</i>

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:49.528 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As the flagship of
 the Imperial Japanese Navy,
 Yamato carries the seal</i>

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:52.231
<i> of the Emperor himself.</i>

00:03:52.764 --> 00:03:55.334
<i> A golden chrysanthemum.</i>

00:03:55.768 --> 00:04:00.273
JAMES: Yamato comes from the
term Yamato-Damashii, which is
the national spirit of Japan.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.911 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
It was literally the
embodiment of Japan itself.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:11.384
<i> NARRATOR: Yamato isn't just
 big, it's also top secret.</i>

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:16.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
ERIC: The Japanese took
enormous measures to keep the
Yamato secret from foreign

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:22.394 line:0%
navies, even to the extent of,
of, of putting up a building
so that western naval attaches

00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:24.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
could not see the ship
under construction.

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:30.503 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Even Yamato's
 commanding officers
 aren't told its true size.</i>

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:37.577
<i> Its secrets, whatever
 they may be, are to be
 protected at all cost.</i>

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:42.415 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARK: And they were
successful in this because
for the entire war,

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:45.451
the Americans had no
idea of the ship's true
size or the size of the

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.720
guns that she carried.

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:53.592
<i> NARRATOR: Japan's secret
 battleships are put to the
 test in one of the biggest</i>

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:56.796
<i> naval battles of World War II.</i>

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:04.003 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Three years after
 Japan bombs Pearl Harbor,</i>

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:06.773 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the Pacific War is
 about to reach its peak.</i>

00:05:07.806 --> 00:05:09.641
<i> Advancing towards Japan,</i>

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.846 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> US and Australian forces
 launch an invasion
 of the Philippines.</i>

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:20.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Yamato and Musashi
 lead the Japanese fleet
 as it tries to stop the</i>

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:23.690
<i> allies in their tracks.</i>

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:33.866 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The Battle of Leyte Gulf in
 October 1944 is the biggest
 naval fight in history.</i>

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:42.241
<i> An epic slugfest of 300 ships.</i>

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:45.878 align:start size:91% position:9%
ERIC: Leyte Gulf
was the last chance the
Japanese fleet had to stop

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:47.980
the American advance.

00:05:50.882 --> 00:05:53.686
<i> NARRATOR: Yamato
 draws first blood.</i>

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Its big guns batter
 the US carrier Gambier Bay,
 sending it to the sea floor.</i>

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:07.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> The Allies
 respond with cannons,
 torpedoes and aircraft.</i>

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:13.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> A massive barrage of
 firepower that destroys
 26 Japanese ships...</i>

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:18.811
<i> and damages dozens more.</i>

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.714 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARK: It marked the end
of the Japanese Navy as an
effective fighting force.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.784
<i> NARRATOR: Yamato is hit...</i>

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:28.755 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> but is saved by its
 immensely strong armor.</i>

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:33.226
<i> Musashi isn't so lucky.</i>

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:40.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> It sinks under
 an onslaught of American
 bombs and torpedoes,</i>

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:45.805 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> and 1,000
 crew members are killed.</i>

00:06:58.183 --> 00:07:03.356
<i> On the brink of defeat,
 the Japanese now resort to
 a new and shocking tactic.</i>

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:08.428
<i> They call it kamikaze.</i>

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:16.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
JAMES: Thousands of young
men dive their aircraft onto
the decks of enemy ships.

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:21.707 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: They believe
 that their self-sacrifice
 will save Japan.</i>

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:30.516
<i> But it's not enough to
 hold back the US forces.</i>

00:07:33.019 --> 00:07:37.390 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> After their victory at
 Leyte Gulf, the Americans
 reached Japan's doorstep,</i>

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:41.194 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> the outlying
 islands of Okinawa.</i>

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:46.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Japan sends Yamato
 into battle once again.</i>

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:50.970
<i> On the 7th of April 1945,</i>

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.773 align:start size:75% position:25%
<i> it powers towards
 its destiny.</i>

00:07:54.674 --> 00:07:58.144 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> With orders to
 intercept the invaders
 and fight to the death.</i>

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:01.581
<i> The ultimate kamikaze mission.</i>

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:05.584
JAMES: This huge ship,
this ultimate battleship,

00:08:05.684 --> 00:08:10.222 align:start size:88% position:13%
is sent on a one-way
mission to turn itself
into a giant gun battery

00:08:10.322 --> 00:08:12.959
to try to keep the
American ships away.

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:18.498 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: But Yamato
 never makes it to Okinawa.</i>

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:24.837
<i> The Americans launch a
 surprise attack, with
 overwhelming force.</i>

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:34.213 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The super battleship's size
 and incredible strength
 can't save it this time.</i>

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:41.720 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Two hours after
 the first wave of bombs,
 the flagship of the</i>

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:46.058
<i> Imperial Japanese Navy
 plunges below the waves.</i>

00:08:48.093 --> 00:08:52.732
<i> So, what were Yamato's
 secrets and why couldn't
 they save the ship?</i>

00:08:57.102 --> 00:08:58.904
<i> Half a century later,</i>

00:08:59.004 --> 00:09:02.909 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> French and Japanese
 wreck explorers set
 out to unlock the mystery.</i>

00:09:06.845 --> 00:09:11.684 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Sonar readings confirm
 wreckage on the seabed
 140 miles south of Japan.</i>

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:17.390 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But the ghostly shadow
 gives little away.</i>

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:21.928
<i> They deploy a submarine.</i>

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:33.039 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> As it descends into
 the darkness, its lights
 pick out a strange shape.</i>

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:49.789 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Could these giant
 twisted pieces of metal
 really belong to Yamato?</i>

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:56.996
<i> They search for proof.</i>

00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:08.407
-Yes.

00:10:10.575 --> 00:10:15.614
JAMES: As one who in the world
shipwreck community watched
those first images come back,

00:10:15.714 --> 00:10:18.951
it was striking and powerful.

00:10:24.256 --> 00:10:27.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Amongst
 the scattered debris...</i>

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:32.465
<i> reminders of the fate of
 the ship's 3,000 man crew.</i>

00:10:35.267 --> 00:10:39.105
<i> Yamato is Japan's
 worst naval tragedy.</i>

00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:49.347 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Using detailed sonar
 data from this expedition,</i>

00:10:49.447 --> 00:10:52.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> along with the latest
 computer imaging technology,</i>

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:56.522
<i> it's possible to drain the
 water from the China Seas,</i>

00:10:58.557 --> 00:11:02.561
<i> to reveal one of the biggest
 wreck sites in the world.</i>

00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:06.298
<i> And finally, discover
 the secrets of Yamato.</i>

00:11:09.668 --> 00:11:13.673
<i> First, the Emperor's seal
 comes clearly into view.</i>

00:11:17.076 --> 00:11:19.778 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> Then, its gigantic
 propellers,</i>

00:11:19.878 --> 00:11:23.182
<i> each stretching
 20 feet across.</i>

00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:30.423 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And shockingly, the final
 agonies of this mammoth
 warship are laid bare.</i>

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:37.063
<i> It is completely ripped apart.</i>

00:11:45.937 --> 00:11:49.874
JAMES: The violent death
of Yamato and the sprawled
corpse of the battleship,

00:11:49.975 --> 00:11:55.981 align:start size:97% position:3%
makes for a compelling image
because the ship was literally
broken apart into chunks,

00:11:56.081 --> 00:11:58.851
and that's what you
see on the bottom.

00:12:01.854 --> 00:12:05.824
<i> NARRATOR: So, what is
 Yamato's big secret?</i>

00:12:06.925 --> 00:12:10.730
<i> Experts scour the site to
 appreciate its sheer scale.</i>

00:12:12.464 --> 00:12:16.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And the incredible thickness
 of its armored hull.</i>

00:12:19.538 --> 00:12:24.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But they believe there's
 something else still hidden,
 and to see it means draining</i>

00:12:24.343 --> 00:12:28.247
<i> not only the ocean,
 but the seabed itself.</i>

00:12:29.949 --> 00:12:33.219
<i> To reveal these.</i>

00:12:36.721 --> 00:12:41.560 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> When Yamato was built,
 the Allies assumed it was
 fitted with 15-inch cannons,</i>

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.663 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> common on battleships
 of the time.</i>

00:12:45.263 --> 00:12:47.732
<i> But they were wrong.</i>

00:12:48.567 --> 00:12:52.704 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Yamato's
 cannons are actually
 18 inches, the biggest and</i>

00:12:52.804 --> 00:12:56.342
<i> most powerful ever
 fitted to a warship.</i>

00:12:56.842 --> 00:13:01.347
<i> Each can fire a shell
 the weight of a car,
 for almost 30 miles.</i>

00:13:06.085 --> 00:13:07.987
JAMES: To have 18-inch
guns on a battleship,

00:13:08.087 --> 00:13:11.991
means not only do you have
bigger guns than other folks,

00:13:12.091 --> 00:13:16.128 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have guns that
can shoot farther, you
have guns that can shoot

00:13:16.228 --> 00:13:18.364
with more punch.

00:13:19.798 --> 00:13:24.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, it's, it's a
tremendous tactical advantage
for the Japanese in the

00:13:24.236 --> 00:13:26.705
age of the battleship.

00:13:33.412 --> 00:13:37.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: Kazushi
 Hiro, a Naval Signalman
 who served on Yamato,</i>

00:13:37.148 --> 00:13:39.919 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> recalls their
 enormous firepower.</i>

00:13:43.488 --> 00:13:45.390 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
KAZUSHI: The sound
was very loud.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:49.228
It felt as if many
thunderbolts struck
the ship all at once.

00:13:54.266 --> 00:13:58.036 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR:
 But if Yamato had the most
 powerful guns on water,</i>

00:13:58.136 --> 00:14:01.106
<i> why didn't it put
 up more of a fight?</i>

00:14:08.781 --> 00:14:11.750 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The drained
 wreck of the world's
 biggest battleship reveals</i>

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.720 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> dramatic details
 of its final battle.</i>

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:21.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Scattered all around,
 hundreds of shell casings
 from Yamato's own guns.</i>

00:14:24.763 --> 00:14:29.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But a closer look
 reveals they're not
 from its giant cannons.</i>

00:14:32.104 --> 00:14:35.040
<i> Many of these casings are
 from a much smaller weapon.</i>

00:14:40.745 --> 00:14:46.718
<i> Yamato's cannons are designed
 to destroy other battleships
 but are of little use against</i>

00:14:46.818 --> 00:14:49.588
<i> fast moving attack aircraft.</i>

00:14:54.025 --> 00:14:57.595
<i> It defends itself with
 much smaller 25-millimeter
 guns that fire shells</i>

00:14:57.696 --> 00:15:00.598
<i> just over an inch in diameter.</i>

00:15:00.832 --> 00:15:03.168
<i> But these guns have a problem.</i>

00:15:04.169 --> 00:15:06.905 line:0%
ERIC: Unlike British
and American capital ships,
which were armed with

00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:11.510
40-millimeter weapons,

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:13.378
the Japanese had
nothing like that.

00:15:13.478 --> 00:15:16.948
They moved straight from
a five-inch anti-aircraft
gun, to a 25-millimeter

00:15:17.048 --> 00:15:21.387 align:start size:91% position:9%
anti-aircraft gun, and
this made Yamato vulnerable
to torpedo bomber attack.

00:15:23.288 --> 00:15:27.759 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
It was a very mediocre
weapon, slow elevation,
slow rate of fire.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:30.930 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it had a lot
of vibrations, so it
was very inaccurate.

00:15:34.098 --> 00:15:38.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Yamato's
 underperforming anti-aircraft
 guns are no match for an</i>

00:15:38.337 --> 00:15:41.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> opponent she was never
 designed to fight.</i>

00:15:46.545 --> 00:15:50.949
<i> Swarms of fast moving
 attack planes, carrying
 bombs and torpedoes,</i>

00:15:51.049 --> 00:15:54.720
<i> launched from the latest
 US aircraft carriers.</i>

00:16:04.429 --> 00:16:07.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Each of these holes marks
 a successful strike.</i>

00:16:11.203 --> 00:16:13.805
<i> Enough to cripple Yamato.</i>

00:16:15.106 --> 00:16:19.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But the drained remains
 of this sunken giant
 reveal another mystery.</i>

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Its 16-inch armor is
 shredded like tin foil,</i>

00:16:26.952 --> 00:16:30.089 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> and its massive
 hull is blown in half.</i>

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:34.426 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> No bomb or torpedo
 could have done that.</i>

00:16:37.829 --> 00:16:43.135
<i> Could it be that Yamato
 was ultimately destroyed
 by her own secret weapon?</i>

00:16:47.505 --> 00:16:52.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Amongst the scattered
 debris lies the heart of the
 ship's fearsome firepower.</i>

00:16:54.412 --> 00:16:59.251 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> This hole is where the forward
 18-inch gun turret used
 to sit and where shells and</i>

00:16:59.351 --> 00:17:04.123 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> propellant were stored
 in magazines before being
 fed to the cannons above.</i>

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:08.393
-This is a potentially very
vulnerable part of the ship.

00:17:08.493 --> 00:17:10.595
If fire is introduced
into the magazine,

00:17:10.695 --> 00:17:14.132
it has the potential to
destroy the entire ship.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: A clue
 to Yamato's fate comes
 from this rare footage.</i>

00:17:20.905 --> 00:17:25.477 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> It shows a torpedoed
 British World War II
 battleship, HMS Barham,</i>

00:17:25.577 --> 00:17:28.913
<i> slowly sinking as
 it takes on water.</i>

00:17:29.948 --> 00:17:34.853 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As the ship capsizes,
 shells begin to tumble from
 the magazine racks inside.</i>

00:17:41.459 --> 00:17:44.830
<i> 800 men die.</i>

00:17:46.598 --> 00:17:51.536
<i> The drained wreck of Yamato
 shows how the blast that
 rips it apart starts here,</i>

00:17:51.636 --> 00:17:55.707
<i> at exactly the position
 of the second magazine.</i>

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:02.881
<i> This is one of the last
 photographs ever taken
 of the super battleship.</i>

00:18:04.816 --> 00:18:08.854
<i> Above it, a column
 of smoke rises three
 miles into the sky.</i>

00:18:09.988 --> 00:18:13.525
<i> The result of that final
 catastrophic explosion.</i>

00:18:15.226 --> 00:18:19.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
-When Yamato blew up, there
was a huge mushroom cloud, a
preview of the mushroom clouds

00:18:19.665 --> 00:18:23.067
that would very soon appear
over Hiroshima and Nagasaki,

00:18:23.501 --> 00:18:25.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
bringing the
Pacific War to an end.

00:18:27.973 --> 00:18:32.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: Kazushi Hiro
 was assigned to another ship
 after his training on Yamato.</i>

00:18:34.679 --> 00:18:39.985
<i> Now aged 94, he still
 prays for his many crew
 mates who died that day.</i>

00:18:45.123 --> 00:18:49.127 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
KAZUSHI: Dozens of
the names of my friends are
inscribed on this cenotaph.

00:18:50.561 --> 00:18:56.902
I come here to visit their
graves once a week since
I have time and I'm alive.

00:19:05.877 --> 00:19:10.015 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Japan's
 top-secret battleship was
 destined for the sea floor.</i>

00:19:11.883 --> 00:19:15.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Not because of its kamikaze
 mission, but its secret
 weapons were rendered useless</i>

00:19:16.088 --> 00:19:19.925
<i> by the changing nature of war.</i>

00:19:22.193 --> 00:19:25.764 align:start size:84% position:16%
-The sinking of
the Yamato was the last
gasp of the battleship.

00:19:26.198 --> 00:19:30.501
It demonstrated that unless
you had air cover, you were
very vulnerable to torpedo

00:19:30.601 --> 00:19:36.275
attack and therefore the
age of the battleship was
certainly coming to an end.

00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:47.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> NARRATOR: The men who
 sacrifice themselves on board
 Yamato are inspired by an</i>

00:19:47.952 --> 00:19:50.388
<i> astonishing event.</i>

00:19:50.755 --> 00:19:54.192
<i> From the age of
 samurai warriors.</i>

00:19:54.492 --> 00:19:59.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> As the China Seas continue
 to drain away, extraordinary
 evidence emerges,</i>

00:19:59.631 --> 00:20:03.669
<i> about an enduring
 maritime mystery.</i>

00:20:06.305 --> 00:20:12.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> How did the mightiest
 invasion fleet of ancient
 times vanish without a trace?</i>

00:20:12.877 --> 00:20:16.148 align:start size:91% position:9%
-It was one of the biggest
disasters in the history
of maritime warfare.

00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:21.753
<i> NARRATOR: Imari Bay, Kyushu.</i>

00:20:23.322 --> 00:20:27.025 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The southernmost of
 Japan's four main islands.</i>

00:20:28.826 --> 00:20:34.666
<i> 700 years ago, warriors of
 the Mongol empire launch
 an invasion of Japan here.</i>

00:20:40.438 --> 00:20:44.643
<i> Under orders from one
 of the most powerful
 leaders of all time...</i>

00:20:45.877 --> 00:20:48.947
<i> Emperor Kublai Khan.</i>

00:20:50.315 --> 00:20:54.052 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> The Emperor is the
 grandson of Genghis Khan,</i>

00:20:54.152 --> 00:20:58.924
<i> the legendary warrior who
 united the nomadic hordes
 of the Mongolian plains.</i>

00:21:01.726 --> 00:21:06.131
<i> Now leading one of the
 largest armies in history,
 Kublai Khan continues</i>

00:21:06.231 --> 00:21:09.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> to expand the
 Mongol empire through China.</i>

00:21:10.502 --> 00:21:15.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> He establishes
 the city of Beijing
 and grows fantastically rich</i>

00:21:15.373 --> 00:21:18.476
<i> from a vast trade network.</i>

00:21:18.576 --> 00:21:23.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> His power over life
 and death stretches from
 Europe to the China Seas.</i>

00:21:24.282 --> 00:21:27.552
<i> But the Great Khan
 isn't satisfied.</i>

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:31.657
-Kublai Khan was expanding
the Mongol empire eastwards.

00:21:31.757 --> 00:21:35.660
He made himself Emperor
of China, he made Korea
into a vassal state,

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:38.463
and now it was Japan's turn.

00:21:38.997 --> 00:21:42.934 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Standing
 against the Khan are the
 samurai warlords of Japan.</i>

00:21:43.668 --> 00:21:48.106 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And in 1281, he
 decides to reckon with
 them once and for all.</i>

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:54.179
<i> He constructs the largest
 fleet ever seen and
 sends it across the sea.</i>

00:21:56.080 --> 00:22:00.284 align:start size:91% position:9%
JAMES: Imagine what it
must have felt like if you
were a Japanese standing

00:22:00.384 --> 00:22:02.720
on the shores there?

00:22:02.987 --> 00:22:08.927
All of a sudden, what you're
seeing is a wall of ships
as far as your eye can see.

00:22:17.302 --> 00:22:19.671
-The Mongol fleet was huge,

00:22:19.771 --> 00:22:23.007
getting on for 4,000 vessels
of various shapes and sizes.

00:22:24.308 --> 00:22:27.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was probably
the biggest amphibious
operation before the

00:22:27.145 --> 00:22:30.148
Normandy D-Day
landings in 1944.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:38.523
<i> NARRATOR: The samurais
 fight bravely, and
 try to hold the line,</i>

00:22:39.724 --> 00:22:42.861 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> but are hopelessly
 outnumbered.</i>

00:22:47.966 --> 00:22:51.302 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> As the first wave of
 invaders battles on shore,</i>

00:22:51.402 --> 00:22:54.339 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> more are approaching
 from the sea.</i>

00:22:55.373 --> 00:22:58.944
<i> But then, something
 astonishing happens.</i>

00:23:00.245 --> 00:23:05.150
<i> The massive fleet of Mongol
 ships mysteriously vanishes.</i>

00:23:07.118 --> 00:23:10.855 align:start size:91% position:9%
-The loss of Kublai Khan's
invasion fleet, getting
on for 4,000 ships,

00:23:10.955 --> 00:23:14.693
was one of the biggest
disasters in the history
of maritime warfare.

00:23:17.995 --> 00:23:20.331
<i> NARRATOR: So, what happened?</i>

00:23:20.431 --> 00:23:24.269 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Japanese scribes credit
 divine intervention.</i>

00:23:27.005 --> 00:23:32.811
<i> Historians, archaeologists,
 and divers scour the seabed
 and pore over the archives.</i>

00:23:35.414 --> 00:23:38.550
<i> But fail to discover
 a single shipwreck.</i>

00:23:39.684 --> 00:23:41.353
<i> Until now.</i>

00:23:49.827 --> 00:23:53.531
<i> NARRATOR: Fascinated with the
 legend of Kublai Khan's fleet,</i>

00:23:53.631 --> 00:23:58.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> maritime archaeologist
 Kenzo Hayashida has
 spent years searching for it.</i>

00:24:02.707 --> 00:24:09.114 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
KENZO: I have committed myself
to the survey since 1988,
so for more than 30 years.

00:24:13.184 --> 00:24:17.322 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Kenzo is inspired
 by an ancient Japanese
 record of the invasion.</i>

00:24:19.157 --> 00:24:22.027
<i> It's only brought out
 on very rare occasions.</i>

00:24:34.772 --> 00:24:38.276
<i> NARRATOR: The scroll clearly
 shows hand to hand combat.</i>

00:24:38.877 --> 00:24:41.813
<i> And even the missing ships.</i>

00:24:43.315 --> 00:24:48.320
<i> But it makes no mention of
 divine intervention plunging
 the fleet to the sea floor.</i>

00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:56.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But under the waters of Imari
 Bay there are some clues.</i>

00:24:58.763 --> 00:25:03.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> A Mongol helmet, buckles,
 even an ancient comb.</i>

00:25:04.002 --> 00:25:07.272
<i> And then, this.</i>

00:25:07.372 --> 00:25:11.443 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> A bronze seal inscribed
 with a date, 1277.</i>

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:18.884
<i> Struck just four years before
 the Mongol invasion and it
 carries the mark of a soldier,</i>

00:25:19.584 --> 00:25:22.219
<i> a very important soldier.</i>

00:25:22.319 --> 00:25:27.392 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
IKUHIRO: It was an official
seal of a company commander
in the Mongolian army,

00:25:27.492 --> 00:25:30.695 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and the discovery became
important evidence.

00:25:32.363 --> 00:25:36.367 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: It's
 the strongest proof yet of
 the Mongol invasion fleet.</i>

00:25:37.001 --> 00:25:41.373
JAMES: In terms of a smoking
gun in archaeology, it doesn't
get much better than that.

00:25:44.242 --> 00:25:47.612
<i> NARRATOR: Inspired
 by the find, Kenzo
 takes to the water.</i>

00:25:49.247 --> 00:25:52.751 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> Layers of thick mud make
 searching here very difficult.</i>

00:25:55.720 --> 00:26:00.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But when he starts dredging
 the sea floor itself, he
 makes his first breakthrough.</i>

00:26:04.962 --> 00:26:08.566
-What we found were mostly
goods carried on the ships.

00:26:08.933 --> 00:26:13.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
For example, weapons,
armor and pots to put food in.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:18.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: But still,
 where are all the ships?</i>

00:26:19.777 --> 00:26:24.416
<i> Kenzo and his team need
 to find a way to peer
 even deeper into the mud.</i>

00:26:25.883 --> 00:26:28.787
<i> And for that, they
 turn to technology.</i>

00:26:48.707 --> 00:26:50.708
<i> NARRATOR: Based on this data,</i>

00:26:50.808 --> 00:26:55.513
<i> draining the coast of
 southern Japan slowly reveals
 something spectacular.</i>

00:26:58.683 --> 00:27:01.853 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> First, some fragments
 of broken timbers.</i>

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:10.061
<i> But then, blowing away
 centuries of silt...</i>

00:27:12.096 --> 00:27:16.501 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> exposes the original
 seabed of 1281.</i>

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:25.443
<i> And there, resting on top
 of it, visible for the
 first time in 700 years,</i>

00:27:27.612 --> 00:27:33.084 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> the extraordinary tangled
 remains of Kublai Khan's
 lost invasion fleet.</i>

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:53.338 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: And the drained
 seabed reveals something else.</i>

00:27:54.939 --> 00:27:59.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
-And there, much like you
would see from a crash
site of an aircraft,

00:27:59.477 --> 00:28:03.581
you see fragments of
ships lying everywhere.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:11.690
<i> NARRATOR: Amongst the
 debris, weapons, pots,</i>

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:17.128
<i> even the bones of
 Mongol warriors.</i>

00:28:20.098 --> 00:28:23.001 align:start size:88% position:13%
-It's as if you took
hundreds of ships and put
them through a blender.

00:28:30.607 --> 00:28:34.378 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: What could wreak
 such havoc on an entire fleet?</i>

00:28:35.246 --> 00:28:39.416
<i> Is this evidence of
 the divine intervention
 of samurai legend?</i>

00:28:40.684 --> 00:28:45.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> The answers lie in the
 thousands of wood fragments
 scattered across the seafloor.</i>

00:28:47.758 --> 00:28:51.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Using the data from
 the sonar scan, like a
 giant 3D jigsaw puzzle,</i>

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:58.636
<i> it's possible to rebuild
 Kublai Khan's warships,
 and it's not what the</i>

00:28:58.736 --> 00:29:01.605
<i> archaeologists expect.</i>

00:29:02.773 --> 00:29:06.678
<i> Most of these ships
 were doomed before
 they even set sail.</i>

00:29:07.946 --> 00:29:10.814
ERIC: Kublai Khan had great
problems mobilizing a fleet.

00:29:10.915 --> 00:29:13.417 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
There were Korean
vessels, but many of
them were just coastal.

00:29:13.517 --> 00:29:16.353 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
There were Chinese
vessels, but many of them
were just river craft.

00:29:16.787 --> 00:29:19.457
They weren't very suitable
for an oceanic invasion.

00:29:21.192 --> 00:29:24.595
<i> NARRATOR: Many of the ships
 lack one critical feature.</i>

00:29:24.695 --> 00:29:27.298
<i> A deep keel.</i>

00:29:27.398 --> 00:29:31.402
<i> Meaning they are unstable and
 easily swamped in rough seas.</i>

00:29:32.636 --> 00:29:35.373 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> What's more,
 they're poorly built.</i>

00:29:59.597 --> 00:30:02.133
<i> NARRATOR: But shoddy
 construction doesn't
 account for the extent</i>

00:30:02.233 --> 00:30:04.836
<i> of the devastation.</i>

00:30:08.405 --> 00:30:12.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> A clue to
 what happened lies 700
 miles away, in Tokyo.</i>

00:30:15.045 --> 00:30:18.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> This is Japan's
 Meteorological Agency.</i>

00:30:19.551 --> 00:30:23.621
<i> Its records only
 go back to the 1950s,
 but in that time,</i>

00:30:23.721 --> 00:30:26.724 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> a clear weather
 pattern has emerged.</i>

00:30:28.559 --> 00:30:32.497 line:0%
HIROSHI: Japan is in the
north west Pacific area,
where typhoons develop most

00:30:32.597 --> 00:30:34.599
frequently in the world.

00:30:37.201 --> 00:30:41.439 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Each year, at
 least a dozen typhoons bear
 down on the Japanese coast.</i>

00:30:45.977 --> 00:30:49.113 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> One thing about the
 Mongol fleet is clear...</i>

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:53.685 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> it arrives in
 late summer of 1281,</i>

00:30:53.952 --> 00:30:56.921
<i> in time for typhoon season.</i>

00:30:57.455 --> 00:31:01.425 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Where wind speeds can
 reach 190 miles per hour,</i>

00:31:01.525 --> 00:31:05.363
<i> generating deadly
 waves 40 feet tall.</i>

00:31:07.532 --> 00:31:10.635 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> A wind the Japanese
 call, kamikaze.</i>

00:31:12.703 --> 00:31:16.140 align:start size:72% position:28%
JAMES:
Kame for God,
Kaze for wind,

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:20.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
creates a powerful
legend of the Divine
Wind that saved Japan,

00:31:20.744 --> 00:31:23.314
or the kamikaze.

00:31:25.416 --> 00:31:27.151
<i> NARRATOR: Japan will
 evoke the Divine Wind</i>

00:31:27.251 --> 00:31:30.855
<i> again on the brink of
 defeat in World War II.</i>

00:31:32.923 --> 00:31:38.229
<i> It doesn't stop US forces
 then, but 700 years earlier,</i>

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:40.698
<i> the original kamikaze smashes</i>

00:31:40.798 --> 00:31:44.636
<i> Kublai Khan's poorly
 built fleet to pieces.</i>

00:31:47.872 --> 00:31:51.276
-Most of the damage would
be due to the typhoon.

00:31:53.578 --> 00:31:56.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> NARRATOR: The greatest
 army of the age...</i>

00:31:58.016 --> 00:32:01.185 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> powerless in the face
 of nature's fury.</i>

00:32:08.492 --> 00:32:13.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> As the China Seas
 continue to drain away, the
 receding water reveals another</i>

00:32:13.631 --> 00:32:19.770
<i> maritime mystery just off
 the coast of Vietnam, a
 vast jumble of shipwrecks</i>

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.974
<i> spanning 2,000 years.</i>

00:32:23.541 --> 00:32:27.545
<i> What made this stretch
 of water one of the most
 dangerous in history?</i>

00:32:28.146 --> 00:32:32.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And what extraordinary
 treasures lie buried
 on the sea floor?</i>

00:32:33.451 --> 00:32:35.820
IAN: First time in history,
eyes are looking at this.

00:32:41.926 --> 00:32:44.462 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The Cham
 Islands, off Vietnam.</i>

00:32:45.129 --> 00:32:48.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Part of a region
 known as the coast
 that sank 1,000 ships.</i>

00:32:51.902 --> 00:32:56.607 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Not the ships of
 fierce invaders, but
 ambitious traders.</i>

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:04.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> Clues to centuries of
 bustling commerce are still
 found on the sea floor here.</i>

00:33:05.883 --> 00:33:10.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> For as long as anyone can
 remember, local divers have
 been making intriguing finds.</i>

00:33:12.156 --> 00:33:15.026
<i> Porcelain and ceramics,
 hundreds of years old.</i>

00:33:17.561 --> 00:33:22.200 line:0%
TRAN XA: According to
archaeologists, the object
is considered antique.

00:33:22.834 --> 00:33:25.870 align:start size:88% position:13%
It belongs to around the
14th or 15th century.

00:33:27.771 --> 00:33:31.843
<i> NARRATOR: In local shops,
 recovered ceramics sell for
 hundreds of dollars apiece.</i>

00:33:33.377 --> 00:33:35.846 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> Where do these
 treasures come from?</i>

00:33:35.946 --> 00:33:39.550
<i> And why are they strewn
 across the harbor floor?</i>

00:33:47.424 --> 00:33:51.562 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> A research team is scanning
 the harbor with the
 latest sonar technology.</i>

00:33:54.831 --> 00:33:56.934 line:0%
IAN: If you go to
a beautiful place like
the Mediterranean,

00:33:57.034 --> 00:33:58.970 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
you've got
40 meters visibility.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:03.540 line:0%
Here we can be down to half
a meter so this time, the
government has allowed us to

00:34:03.641 --> 00:34:07.445
bring remote sensing
equipment, and we're
using side-scan sonar.

00:34:09.313 --> 00:34:12.183 align:start size:88% position:13%
First time in history
eyes are looking at this.

00:34:13.084 --> 00:34:16.654
<i> NARRATOR: Just minutes
 into the search, grainy
 images of what could be</i>

00:34:16.754 --> 00:34:19.857 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> wreck sites appear
 on the screen.</i>

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:21.659 align:start size:81% position:19%
-Guys, come and
have a look at this.

00:34:21.759 --> 00:34:23.961
This is really interesting.

00:34:26.764 --> 00:34:29.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: The team
 dives for a closer look.</i>

00:34:31.535 --> 00:34:35.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> And in the
 gloom, a field of broken
 ceramics slowly emerges.</i>

00:34:40.177 --> 00:34:46.284
<i> They scour a total area of
 four square miles and what
 they find is extraordinary.</i>

00:34:47.351 --> 00:34:50.655
-Ooh, hello baby.

00:34:54.425 --> 00:34:57.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> NARRATOR: After careful
 analysis of the survey data...</i>

00:34:58.496 --> 00:35:02.733 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> it's now possible
 to drain the sea
 from the Cham Islands.</i>

00:35:06.571 --> 00:35:11.509
<i> And remove layers of
 sand and mud to reveal
 an astounding sight.</i>

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:17.815
<i> A treasure trove of 15th
 century Chinese ceramics.</i>

00:35:18.782 --> 00:35:25.056
<i> Cups, bowls, plates,
 still stacked in neat
 rows after 600 years.</i>

00:35:30.127 --> 00:35:34.899 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Scattered all around are
 more wrecks, as many as 30.</i>

00:35:37.901 --> 00:35:42.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're talking 2,000
odd years at least of wrecks
that we'll actually have

00:35:42.573 --> 00:35:44.909
under the surface here.

00:35:45.776 --> 00:35:47.979
<i> NARRATOR: Why so many wrecks?</i>

00:35:48.079 --> 00:35:51.249 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> And why so many
 loads of porcelain?</i>

00:35:53.283 --> 00:35:57.020
<i> The answer lies in the Cham
 Islands' unique position,</i>

00:35:57.120 --> 00:36:01.024 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> at the eastern
 end of the world's
 greatest trade route,</i>

00:36:01.324 --> 00:36:04.862
<i> the Maritime Silk Road.</i>

00:36:04.962 --> 00:36:09.534
<i> Stretching from Asia to
 the Middle East through to
 Constantinople and Europe.</i>

00:36:11.201 --> 00:36:17.274 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> For 1,000 years,
 this oceanic superhighway
 connects princes and popes,</i>

00:36:17.374 --> 00:36:20.377
<i> kingdoms and empires.</i>

00:36:20.478 --> 00:36:25.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
JAMES: It thrived because
you had silks, you had spices,
but you also had porcelain.

00:36:29.153 --> 00:36:32.356 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
Chinese porcelains
are beautiful,
they're magnificent.

00:36:34.525 --> 00:36:37.194
-It's simply down to the
clays the Chinese had.

00:36:37.294 --> 00:36:40.665 align:start size:84% position:16%
They had very,
very white clays and
it was unlike anything

00:36:40.765 --> 00:36:43.801
anybody had seen before.

00:36:47.538 --> 00:36:49.807 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: The drained
 harbor of the Cham Islands</i>

00:36:49.907 --> 00:36:53.043 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> reveals yet more
 spectacular sights.</i>

00:36:53.710 --> 00:36:56.881
<i> A United Nations
 of shipwrecks.</i>

00:36:58.081 --> 00:36:59.783
<i> A Spanish galleon.</i>

00:36:59.883 --> 00:37:03.187
<i> A Chinese junk,
 an Arabian dhow.</i>

00:37:04.455 --> 00:37:08.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But why are so many ships
 wrecked in such a small area?</i>

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:13.330 align:start size:91% position:9%
-I like to call this
the parking lot theory
because most car accidents

00:37:13.431 --> 00:37:16.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
happen when you're
trying to get in and
out of the parking lot,

00:37:16.433 --> 00:37:19.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the same thing
holds true for harbors.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:23.341
You have a cluster of ships
trying to get in, get out,
and if the wind changes,

00:37:23.441 --> 00:37:26.277 align:start size:91% position:9%
you're blown right up onto
the rocks of the beach.

00:37:28.679 --> 00:37:32.616 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: Drawn by the
 lure of porcelain and silk,
 the crews of these ships</i>

00:37:32.716 --> 00:37:35.519 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> all hoped to
 make their fortune.</i>

00:37:36.553 --> 00:37:40.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But instead,
 they paid the ultimate
 price on this congested</i>

00:37:40.357 --> 00:37:43.494
<i> and storm-lashed coast.</i>

00:37:46.363 --> 00:37:50.134
<i> Today, the China Seas are
 still a major trade route,</i>

00:37:51.268 --> 00:37:55.672 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> but it's not porcelain
 that's transported
 across the seas, it's data.</i>

00:37:56.573 --> 00:38:01.612
<i> What was once the Great
 Silk Highway, is now an
 Information Superhighway.</i>

00:38:05.949 --> 00:38:08.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> And just like
 the Maritime Silk Road,</i>

00:38:08.886 --> 00:38:12.390 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> this Data Highway
 is beset with dangers.</i>

00:38:15.091 --> 00:38:19.263 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> What are the deep-sea
 risks to the Worldwide Web?</i>

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:22.066 align:start size:91% position:9%
GRAHAM: In this particular
case, effectively
it was a perfect storm.

00:38:29.506 --> 00:38:34.211 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Our love of
 the internet knows no bounds,
 with demand for data doubling</i>

00:38:34.311 --> 00:38:36.414
<i> every two years.</i>

00:38:37.248 --> 00:38:40.050
<i> And the biggest
 gigabyte gluttons?</i>

00:38:40.150 --> 00:38:42.720
<i> Social media and video.</i>

00:38:43.854 --> 00:38:46.123
<i> Lots of video.</i>

00:38:46.223 --> 00:38:50.294
GRAHAM: Currently, there
are four million views of
YouTube videos per minute.

00:38:52.496 --> 00:38:55.766 line:0%
That is an enormous amount of
data that's being consumed.

00:38:57.201 --> 00:39:00.204
<i> NARRATOR: Add to that
 the trillions of dollars
 in electronic funds</i>

00:39:00.304 --> 00:39:03.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> that shuttle between
 corporations and banks...</i>

00:39:04.841 --> 00:39:09.913 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> and it amounts to an
 astonishing 3.4 petabits
 of data that we gobble up,</i>

00:39:10.013 --> 00:39:13.084
<i> every 60 seconds.</i>

00:39:13.484 --> 00:39:16.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
-And for those who don't
know what a petabit
is, ten to the 15 zeros.

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:25.096
<i> NARRATOR: All that data
 has to be stored somewhere,
 which we call the cloud.</i>

00:39:28.399 --> 00:39:32.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But data doesn't
 travel up into the sky.</i>

00:39:33.036 --> 00:39:36.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> It's actually transported
 deep below the sea.</i>

00:39:39.476 --> 00:39:43.413
<i> Draining all the oceans
 reveals the million-mile
 network that connects</i>

00:39:43.513 --> 00:39:46.717
<i> the Worldwide Web.</i>

00:39:48.852 --> 00:39:52.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Where data shoots
 across the sea floor
 at the speed of light,</i>

00:39:52.723 --> 00:39:55.892 align:start size:78% position:22%
<i> inside vast
 arteries of cables.</i>

00:39:55.992 --> 00:39:57.995
JAYNE: We talk
about the cloud.

00:39:58.095 --> 00:40:04.234 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
The reality is 98% of
the internet runs over fiber
optic cables under the ocean.

00:40:05.869 --> 00:40:09.372 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> NARRATOR: One of the
 busiest and most important
 stretches of the internet</i>

00:40:09.473 --> 00:40:12.142
<i> is here under the China Seas,</i>

00:40:12.242 --> 00:40:15.913
<i> in the Luzon Strait, between
 Taiwan and the Philippines.</i>

00:40:16.514 --> 00:40:21.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> And like all undersea cables,
 this Data Superhighway
 is vulnerable to attack.</i>

00:40:24.054 --> 00:40:28.392 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> From the ocean's most
 fearsome predator,
 biting onto cables...</i>

00:40:29.893 --> 00:40:34.030
<i> to entanglements with
 even bigger creatures.</i>

00:40:35.665 --> 00:40:39.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> This footage shows what
 happens when a humpback whale
 becomes wrapped in a loose</i>

00:40:40.003 --> 00:40:43.407
<i> data cable off the
 coast of Norway.</i>

00:40:44.575 --> 00:40:49.447 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> The fire department manages
 to free the whale, but
 it means cutting the cable,</i>

00:40:51.115 --> 00:40:54.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> plunging parts of Scandinavia
 into an offline abyss.</i>

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:05.695 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> To reduce the dangers,
 engineers take a course not
 open to ancient mariners,</i>

00:41:05.795 --> 00:41:10.400
<i> burying precious cables
 deep below the seabed.</i>

00:41:11.668 --> 00:41:16.573 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> This delicate work
 is performed by a flotilla of
 specialized engineering ships,</i>

00:41:18.075 --> 00:41:22.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> which slowly unwind enormous
 drums of cable that will
 stretch for thousands of miles</i>

00:41:22.912 --> 00:41:26.350
<i> across the oceans.</i>

00:41:27.017 --> 00:41:31.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> But the crucial work,
 happens deep below the waves.</i>

00:41:38.094 --> 00:41:42.799 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Draining the water from
 above the ocean bed exposes
 the giant machines that</i>

00:41:42.899 --> 00:41:46.269
<i> build the Worldwide Web.</i>

00:41:46.903 --> 00:41:51.075
<i> A plough, the size of a house,
 crawls along the sea floor.</i>

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:59.450 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> High pressure water
 jets help carve a trench
 ten feet deep and the</i>

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:02.653
<i> cable is buried inside it.</i>

00:42:08.658 --> 00:42:14.030
<i> In the deepest parts of the
 ocean, far beyond the reach
 of anchors or even whales,</i>

00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:18.736
<i> the engineers lay cables
 directly on the seabed.</i>

00:42:22.238 --> 00:42:28.011
<i> Draining the deepest canyon of
 the Luzon Strait reveals the
 main data artery that connects</i>

00:42:28.111 --> 00:42:34.084 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Asia and the West,
 19 cables that shuttle
 trillions of gigabytes of</i>

00:42:34.184 --> 00:42:39.623 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> data between businesses,
 governments, universities,
 and stock markets,</i>

00:42:41.959 --> 00:42:45.329 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> all of them lying
 exposed and vulnerable.</i>

00:42:48.064 --> 00:42:51.235 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> Graham Evans is
 the Managing Director of
 a company that plans where</i>

00:42:51.335 --> 00:42:54.972 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> cables are threaded
 across the globe's oceans.</i>

00:42:56.573 --> 00:42:59.743 align:start size:84% position:16%
-It's not just a flat,
featureless seabed.

00:43:01.978 --> 00:43:06.250
Some of it is extremely rugged
and we're having to maneuver
around some of these features.

00:43:09.853 --> 00:43:13.724
<i> NARRATOR: His job is to
 find the shortest route
 from point A to point B.</i>

00:43:15.826 --> 00:43:19.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> But avoiding underwater
 obstacles mean that
 cables converge to what's</i>

00:43:19.362 --> 00:43:22.466
<i> known as a Choke Point.</i>

00:43:24.334 --> 00:43:28.839
<i> Like the Luzon Strait
 and its 19 cables
 linking east and west.</i>

00:43:30.607 --> 00:43:32.375
-Why is it a choke point?

00:43:32.475 --> 00:43:35.778
There are no alternative
routes when you're trying
to go from Asia to the

00:43:35.879 --> 00:43:39.449
United States,
or Asia to Japan.

00:43:40.751 --> 00:43:45.956
<i>NARRATOR: On December 26, 2006,
the choke point becomes choked.</i>

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:50.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
-It was certainly
a, a date that the
industry won't forget.

00:43:53.897 --> 00:43:58.836 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: At 8:26 pm,
 a deep rumble shakes the
 seabed of the China Seas,</i>

00:44:00.604 --> 00:44:04.441
<i> an earthquake with
 a magnitude of 7.1.</i>

00:44:12.882 --> 00:44:19.056 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> In Taiwan, buildings
 collapse, two people are
 killed, and dozens injured.</i>

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:25.095
<i> But that's not the end
 of the devastation.</i>

00:44:25.996 --> 00:44:30.267 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> In the deep canyon of
 the Luzon Strait, there's a
 second stage to the quake.</i>

00:44:32.936 --> 00:44:36.540 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> A massive underwater
 landslide.</i>

00:44:39.242 --> 00:44:42.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
-In this particular case,
it was a, effectively
it was a perfect storm.

00:44:43.480 --> 00:44:46.616
There had been this huge
build up of sediment at
the head of this canyon,

00:44:46.716 --> 00:44:49.353
and it didn't need
much to trigger it.

00:44:49.453 --> 00:44:53.624
<i> NARRATOR: Directly in
 its path, the data cables
 on the canyon floor.</i>

00:44:54.024 --> 00:44:57.060 align:start size:91% position:9%
-So, if you're
on the floor of the canyon,
what you would see is this

00:44:57.160 --> 00:45:00.764
billowing mass of black mud.

00:45:05.134 --> 00:45:08.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
It pretty well took out
every single cable that went
between southeast Asia and

00:45:08.905 --> 00:45:11.408
the United States.

00:45:18.448 --> 00:45:22.619 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR: The internet
 shuts down and within
 minutes millions feel the</i>

00:45:22.719 --> 00:45:26.056
<i> pain in their wallets.</i>

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:29.092
-No submarine cables, no cash.

00:45:29.359 --> 00:45:31.428
<i> NARRATOR: ATMs stop working.</i>

00:45:31.528 --> 00:45:33.797
<i> Stock trading halts.</i>

00:45:33.897 --> 00:45:37.301
<i> Billions of dollars of
 transactions freeze.</i>

00:45:39.168 --> 00:45:43.507
<i> Corporations and banks
 are paralyzed on both
 sides of the Pacific.</i>

00:45:44.574 --> 00:45:48.311
-In this particular case,
there was never so many
simultaneous cable breaks

00:45:48.411 --> 00:45:53.383
that had such a devastating
impact on the world's
ability to use the internet.

00:45:55.618 --> 00:45:58.622 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> NARRATOR:
 After some frantic work,
 data is rerouted via</i>

00:45:58.722 --> 00:46:02.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
<i> other networks,
 eventually stemming the chaos.</i>

00:46:04.094 --> 00:46:08.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> But it takes three
 months to fully repair
 all the broken lines.</i>

00:46:11.201 --> 00:46:13.470 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> This cable crew
 and others like them,</i>

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:17.373 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> will be busy
 for decades to come as the
 demand for data continues</i>

00:46:17.473 --> 00:46:22.746
<i> to surge, driven by the
 booming economies of Asia.</i>

00:46:27.250 --> 00:46:30.721 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> To compete, or collaborate,
 trade or invade?</i>

00:46:32.755 --> 00:46:36.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> These twin human
 instincts continue to
 define the China Seas.</i>

00:46:40.897 --> 00:46:43.967 line:0%
<i> Draining the waters here
 reveals a turbulent past.</i>

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:50.040 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> Exposes the truth behind
 a mysterious legend.</i>

00:46:54.377 --> 00:46:58.315 line:0%
<i> Celebrates the wonder
 of the Worldwide Web.</i>

00:47:00.884 --> 00:47:05.522 line:0%
<i> And whatever's to come in
 the next digital revolution.</i>

00:47:06.523 --> 00:47:07.591 line:0%
Captioned by Cotter
Captioning Services.

